What is the use of registering variables in C language. What is the use of using "static" keywords to declare variables? What are the difference?

Answer

Variables of type static have the same value when called multiple times. This is in contrast to a variable that is re-initialized on each call to a function.

Register variables are hints to the compiler to try to keep the variables in registers. The compiler may or may not obey your hint. It is also the case that the compiler can optimize your code without register variables being present.

I recommend that you ignore register variables as they are a waste of programming time.

The static variable can help to maintain some information across multiple calls. This is in contrast to non-static variables which are re-initialized on each call.

For a function call, local variables are usually allocated in the stack. If the function is called multiple times then multiple copies of the local variable can exist. This is because when the stack pointer is incremented to allocate a variable then the old value is lost.

If you declare a variable as static then the compiler will allocate space for the variable when the program starts and when the program exits it will deallocate that space. Since the space allocated to the static variable is not destroyed when the function completes execution, its value is preserved across function calls.

Example:

int foo() {
static int var = 0;
var += 10;
return var;
}

If you call foo() multiple times you will get 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 etc. If you declare the variable as int var = 0 instead of static int var = 0 then the result will be 1

